WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Coraline first encounters The Cat as she explores&nbsp;
the grounds of her new home, where she sees it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.280
sitting on walls and tree stumps. At first,&nbsp;
she tried to play with it, but whenever she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.240
approached, it ran away. But the two would soon be&nbsp;
forced to work together to counter a very powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.960
ancient evil known as The Beldam. The Cat has&nbsp;
become one of the most infamous and mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.600
characters among Coraline fans, the subject&nbsp;
of countless wild theories and speculations.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.720
– The cat is the reincarnation&nbsp;
of the Beldam’s ex-lover.&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.160
– How there’s possibly two cats trapped in&nbsp;
one body, but both of them are not real cats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.440
One of them is the spirit of Wybie’s grandma’s&nbsp;
father, and the other is either the son of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.800
other mother or the spirit of the other world.
– Even if The Cat does work for the Beldam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.360
he could still be related to the Lovat family.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.920
– While I do find those ideas a little bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.440
uh, what's the right word? Unhinged! I do have my&nbsp;
own ideas about who this feisty feline really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.880
so to explore those, let’s take a look at&nbsp;
the history of the cat from the Coraline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.800
novel. To learn about the cat’s connections to&nbsp;
witchcraft, why he can talk in the Beldam’s world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.560
and why The Beldam fears this creature,&nbsp;
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.576
This video is sponsored by Audible.

00:01:06.576 --> 00:01:12.150
(metal music)

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.880
Welcome to Horror History. Although I’m&nbsp;
sure you don’t need a Horror History lesson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.040
seeing as how you’re the expert on such things.&nbsp;
Today we’re talking about the cat from the novel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.920
Coraline, however, this cat may have actually&nbsp;
had an appearance before that… retroactively.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.720
The movie adaptation of Coraline is directed by&nbsp;
Henry Selick, who also directed the 1993 movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.000
The Nightmare Before Christmas. Both&nbsp;
movies are stop-motion animations so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.560
they probably reused the same model, or made a&nbsp;
very similar model in Coraline as an easter egg.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.200
In Coraline, the Cat gets a voice courtesy of&nbsp;
voice actor Keith David. David has a very deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.440
silky voice; he’s usually known for playing&nbsp;
some kind of monster or badass mercenary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.400
but he’ll occasionally voice a creepy cat, like&nbsp;
in Once Upon a Time in Wonderland or Coraline.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.240
To unravel the mystery of the black cat,&nbsp;
we first need to understand its history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:58.960
so let's take it back to before&nbsp;
Coraline entered the picture.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.560
(spooky music)

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.560
Like Coraline, there is only one cat, who&nbsp;
travels back and forth between the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.560
world and the Other World, the realm created&nbsp;
by the Beldam to try to trick children into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.400
falling into her trap. For this reason, The&nbsp;
Cat is the only character from Coraline’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.480
world who has no Other World counterpart.&nbsp;
Between this, and the fact that the cat can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.480
talk while in the Beldam’s world, I don’t&nbsp;
think that this cat was always just a cat.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.320
One of the more prominent examples of a black&nbsp;
cat in culture comes from Greek Mythology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.240
Galinthias was a servant of the Greek Goddess,&nbsp;
Hera. Hera wanted to prevent the birth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:35.920
this dude named Heracles, but Galinthias&nbsp;
ruined this plan by saying that the baby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.240
had already been born, so as punishment,&nbsp;
Hera turned Galinthias into a black cat.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:44.160
It’s possible something along these lines happened&nbsp;
before the events of Coraline. The cat was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.400
probably a human who snuck into the Other World&nbsp;
and interfered with The Beldam’s plans to steal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:52.800
the soul of a kid. At one point, he even tells&nbsp;
Coraline that he walked into The Other World, just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.440
like she did. Unable to steal his soul, and unable&nbsp;
to get her claws on him to kill him, The Beldam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.760
was only able to use her magic to turn him into a&nbsp;
cat. Like the Ghost Children, who I discussed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.640
previous Horror History, details of his old life,&nbsp;
like his name, faded from memory as time passed.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.000
In the movie, we even see that the Cat has a&nbsp;
chunk of his ear missing, as if that was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.080
only body part that The Beldam was able to get&nbsp;
her sharp claws on before he scampered away.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.520
When Coraline encounters the Cat, in the Other&nbsp;
World for the first time, he wishes her a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.200
afternoon and she thinks “its voice sounded&nbsp;
like the voice at the back of (her) head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.120
the voice she thought words in, but a man’s&nbsp;
voice, not a girl’s.” So if the voice didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.480
actually sound like her, she must be talking&nbsp;
about the cadence, which is part of why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.240
I think that the cat was once someone who&nbsp;
wandered into the Other World, like Coraline.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.800
But there’s a lot more to it than that, I swear.&nbsp;
I’m not one of those unhinged Coraline YouTubers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.960
In the ancient Greek tales, Galinthias was&nbsp;
taken in by another Greek deity, Hecate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.640
the Goddess of witchcraft and death. This is&nbsp;
likely the origin of the trope that’s still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.560
with us today, where black cats and witches&nbsp;
are commonly associated with each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.640
I’ve talked a little bit in my other videos about&nbsp;
how Coraline herself has witch-like properties.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.640
When Ms. Spink reads Coraline’s tea leaves, the&nbsp;
two neighbor-women determine she has danger in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.560
her future and give her a stone with a hole in&nbsp;
it. They don’t explicitly describe what it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:03.840
but it’s clearly supposed to be an adder&nbsp;
stone, sometimes known as a witch stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.280
an artifact said to give the user magic&nbsp;
protections. When Coraline looks through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:10.640
her dresser in The Other World, one of the&nbsp;
outfits she discovers is a witch costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.560
and her father has a song about her where he&nbsp;
refers to her as his “twitchy-witchy girl”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:19.680
In the movie, Coraline even uses a magic dowsing&nbsp;
rod and Wybie refers to her as a water witch.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.120
– Well, great to meet a Michigan water witch.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.880
– Coraline doesn’t get along with the cat much&nbsp;
at first. He’s described as a haughty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.440
black cat, meaning he’s arrogantly superior&nbsp;
and disdainful. He has no satisfying answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.960
for her as to why he can talk and when she&nbsp;
tells him that cats can’t talk at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.560
he sarcastically states that she’s&nbsp;
the expert, and he’s only a cat.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.080
Coraline tells him that they could be&nbsp;
friends, but he responds by telling her:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.720
“We could be rare specimens of an exotic breed&nbsp;
of African dancing elephants. But we’re not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:50.640
At least,” it added cattily, after darting&nbsp;
a brief look at Coraline, “I’m not.”

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.240
Before this first conversation ends, she&nbsp;
asks the cat what his name is. He insists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.520
that cats do not have names, they know&nbsp;
who they are. We’ve seen something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.360
this before here in Horror History. When I&nbsp;
covered Valak, from the Conjuring franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:05.600
I explained how in religions like Christianity,&nbsp;
naming a creature gives a person dominion over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.160
it. Lorraine Warren needs to discover Valak’s name&nbsp;
in order to address it and banish it back to hell.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.880
Witches and by association, their black cats,&nbsp;
are often seen as evil or threatening in major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.880
religions. So by not having a name, The Cat&nbsp;
makes it harder for anyone to gain power over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.880
it by calling or summoning it. There’s even a&nbsp;
conversation later where The Cat points out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.680
calling for a cat is pointless, akin to calling a&nbsp;
whirlwind. Black cats have a long history of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.920
linked to the supernatural or spiritual. Ancient&nbsp;
Egyptians worshiped the Goddess Bastet, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.320
had a black cat’s head, and they built huge cat&nbsp;
monuments. During the middle ages, when the major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.560
religions we know today were becoming dominant,&nbsp;
witchcraft was seen as a sign of anti-religion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.920
and cats as a sign of bad luck. More recently,&nbsp;
Mayday Parade described a black cat who planned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.800
burn its city down with a backpack of fireworks.&nbsp;
Sounds terrifying, this is why dogs are better.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:54.640
The cat commends Coraline on bringing the&nbsp;
protective adder stone, and disappears behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.640
some trees. He seems to have the ability to travel&nbsp;
through channels between the two worlds that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.800
Coraline can’t see or perceive. He literally&nbsp;
knows all the ins and outs of this world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.960
suggesting he’s been traveling back and forth&nbsp;
for some time. This is probably the reason he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.400
was able to discover when Coraline’s&nbsp;
parents were kidnapped by The Beldam.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.160
After Mr. and Mrs. Jones go missing, he&nbsp;
greets Coraline on her bed as she wakes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.360
and coaxes her to follow him to the hallway&nbsp;
mirror where she can see that her Mom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.880
Dad are trapped beyond a foggy glass.&nbsp;
She asks if he knows where they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.800
and he blinks at her, as if to say “yes”.&nbsp;
This is something that happens a few times;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.160
The Cat blinks to say yes when in the real&nbsp;
world. There’s actually a little easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:31.200
egg at the beginning of the movie to reference&nbsp;
this, which I don’t think I’ve mentioned before.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.880
– [Coraline] I’m just looking for an old well. Know it?

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.680
– [Zac] As they cross back into The Other World, Coraline&nbsp;
tells a story about bravery, in which her father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.920
protected her from a swarm of wasps when she was&nbsp;
younger. The cat asks if that’s why she’s going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.080
back, to try to save them, seemingly not really&nbsp;
understanding the point of the story. This may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.880
playing on the idea of cats often being depicted&nbsp;
as timid. They are the inspiration for slang terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.760
used to describe a timid person, like scaredy cat&nbsp;
or pussy cat. Knowing what the Other Mother truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.400
is, The Cat will go in and out of the Other World,&nbsp;
but doesn’t seem keen on confronting her himself.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:08.160
As they cross the threshold, Coraline&nbsp;
remarks that her companion is talking again.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.080
“How fortunate I am,” said the cat, “in&nbsp;
having a traveling companion of such wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.680
and intelligence.” Its tone remained&nbsp;
sarcastic, but its fur was bristling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.240
and its brush of a tail stuck up in the air.&nbsp;
The two were entering the lair of the beast.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.560
(impact)

